A||||2||GarethWright||Gettin Warm on the Highway||||1||
Z||000000||GarethWright||06-20-2001||02:00 AM||||Hello all,<BR>My 87gt is getting a tad toasty at highway speeds, normal operating temp was about on the Big mark between 100 and 220, but on the highway with AC on, temp goes up over 220, until I crank down the windows and turn on the vents until it cools back down.  I took the thermostat out for a temporary fix (cycles all the coolant constantly right?) so now it runs a little cooler around town, but still gets HOT on the highway, it just takes about twice as long to get hot as it did with the thermostat in.  Is this a radiator problem, and if so, what are my options?  I haven't noticed it leaking and coolant anywhere.<BR>Thanks in advance<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000001||84Bill||06-20-2001||08:50 AM||||Hummm see if your radiator fan is spinning backwards (blowing out the front) when the engine is hot. Check the Ogre's cave link bellow and click on engine cooling on how to make sure your cooling system is free from air bubbles. You could have a radiator that is getting clogged, if everything else is ok. There have been rare occasions where the waterpump impeller is cavitating and causing low flow..    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-20-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000002||Formula88||06-20-2001||09:29 AM||||My guess would be a coolant flow problem.  Either crimped coolant pipes along the sides of the car, air bubbles in the cooling system, or maybe an airflow problem, like was suggested.  Make sure your fan is running (correct direction) and getting good air flow, check your pipes for crimps, and then try to bleed your cooling system  to make sure their's no air in it.||||reg||1
